   "id": "c9_learn_english_concepts",
   "type": "contentAudio",
   "mode": "topicPages",
   "title": "Learn English Concepts",
   "topics": [
    {
     "id": "definitions",
     "title": "Definitions",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "para",
       "text": "There are two voices in English. The [[u]]active voice[[/u]] is where the subject of the sentence does the action. \"Zach hits the ball.\""
      },
      {
       "type": "para",
       "text": "The [[u]]passive voice[[/u]] is where the subject is acted on by the verb. \"Zach was hit by the ball.\"",
       "gapBefore": true
      },
      {
       "type": "para",
       "text": "Greek adds a third voice, the [[u]]middle voice[[/u]], which we will look at shortly.",
       "gapBefore": true
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "identifyingTraits",
     "title": "Identifying Traits",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "para",
       "text": "A passive verb often can be identified by placing a \"by what\" after the verb."
      },
      {
       "type": "para",
       "text": "\"Zach is hit by the ball.\"",
       "gapBefore": true
      },
      {
       "type": "para",
       "text": "\"Zach is hit by what?\" — the ball.",
       "gapBefore": true
      },
      {
       "type": "para",
       "text": "Zach is the subject being acted on. The ball is the agent doing the action.",
       "gapBefore": true
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "translation",
     "title": "Translation",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "para",
       "text": "The present tense may describe punctiliar action (single point in time: He hit the ball) or continuous action (He is hitting the ball). When the passive is used a helping verb carries the tense of the verb."
      },
      {
       "type": "para",
       "text": "He [[u]]is[[/u]] hit by the ball. — present punctiliar\nHe [[u]]is being[[/u]] hit by the ball — present cont.\nHe [[u]]was[[/u]] hit by the ball. — past\nHe [[u]]will be[[/u]] hit by the ball. — future",
       "gapBefore": true
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "c9_learn_mp_verbs",
   "type": "contentAudio",
   "mode": "topicPages",
   "title": "Learn Present Middle/Passive Verbs",
   "topics": [
    {
     "id": "introduction",
     "title": "Introduction",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "para",
       "text": "The present middle and passive have exactly the same form. The context must be examined to determine which is being used. There are approximately three times as many passive as middle verbs in the New Testament. A helping verb is used when translating passives. The helping verb indicates whether the verb is being taken in a [[link:punctiliar]]punctiliar[[/link]] or [[link:continuous]]continuous[[/link]] manner."
      },
      {
       "type": "para",
       "text": "The middle has several functions:",
       "gapBefore": true
      },
      {
       "type": "numbered",
       "items": [
        "[[u]]deponent:[[/u]] where the middle is translated as active. Most middles (75%) are deponent and should be translated as an active. \"Tanya splashed Rebekah.\"",
        "the subject does an action on the object but it in turn impacts the subject. (Mounce, pp. 149, 224)",
        "self-interest \"Tanya splashed herself.\""
       ],
       "gapBefore": true
      }
     ]
    },
